<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write a rule that describes these input-output pairs:</item>

    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> For example: Function \(F\) takes a word for its input and gives the number of letters in that word for its output.</para>
  <item>Here are some input-output pairs with the same inputs but different outputs:</item>
    <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> For example: Function \(v\) takes a word for its input and gives the number of vowels in that word as its output.</para>
</list>

<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>If a student wonders what happens to a person born on February 29, tell them that the output of the function is the original birth date, not the annual birthday.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Discuss with students:</para>
<list>
  <item>“Why is G a function, but P isn’t?” (Each input for G has a unique output, while inputs for P may have several outputs. For example, 2020 is the high school graduation date for Storm Reid, Finn Wolfhard, Skai Jackson, Lil Tecca, and Bryce Young. 2012 is the high school graduation date of both Drake and Ariana Grande.)</item>
  <item>“Would it be acceptable to express relationship P using function notation, for instance: \(P\)(2021) = Olivia Rodrigo? Why or why not?” (No, because this notation is reserved for functions.)</item>
</list>
<para>Some students might wonder if G is still a function if multiple people have the same name. For instance, there might be a few people named Sophia Smith, and if we enter “Sophia Smith” as the input for G, we would likely get different birthdays for the output.</para>
<para>Acknowledge that this is true, and that G would only be a function if it assumes that no two people have the same full name, or if another identifier could be used to distinguish people with the same first name and last name (for instance, if a middle name or initial is also used, or if a number is added to each Sophia Smith to distinguish them from one another).</para>

<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para><title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para><title>MLR 2 Collect and Display: Conversing</title></para>

  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
      <para>Listen for and collect vocabulary, gestures, and diagrams students use to describe what makes a relationship between two variables a function. Capture student language that reflects a variety of ways to describe the characteristics of and differences between a relationship that is a function and a relationship that is not a function. Amplify the words “unique,” “input,” and “output.” Write the students’ words on a visual display and update it throughout the remainder of the lesson. Remind students to borrow language from the display as needed. This will help students read and use mathematical language during their partner and whole-group discussions.</para>
      <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Maximize meta-awareness; Support sense-making</para>
      <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title">Provide support for students</para>
      <para>
        <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3765a3cff09304aea8d8db39295b1c00ffd0c12f">Distribute graphic organizers</link>
           to the students to assist them with participating in this routine.
      </para>
</div>
</div>



<title> 4.2.3: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
 <para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
 <para class="os-raise-text-bold"> The price for pizzas, in dollars, is a function of the number of pizzas bought, \(n\).</para>
        <para>Identify the input, output, and meaning of the statement:</para>
 <para>\(p(11)=55\)</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
       Answer
      </entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
       Feedback
      </entry>
	  </row></thead><tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>Input: 55</para>
        <para>Output: 11</para>
        <para>11 pizzas cost $55</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The meaning is correct, but the input and output are not correct. The input is the number of pizzas. The answer is: Input: 11, Output: 55, 11 pizzas cost $55.</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>Input: 11</para>
        <para>Output: 55</para>
        <para>11 pizzas cost $55</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>That’s correct! Check yourself: The price depends on the number of pizzas bought.</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>Input: 55</para>
        <para>Output: 11</para>
        <para>55 pizzas cost $11</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The input and output are switched, and the meaning statement is also switched. The input is the number of pizzas. The answer is: Input: 11, Output: 55, 11 pizzas cost $55.</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        <para>Input: 11</para>
        <para>Output: 55</para>
        <para>55 pizzas cost $11</para>
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The input and output are correct, but the meaning statement is not correct. The answer is: Input: 11, Output: 55, 11 pizzas cost $55.</para>
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<title>4.2.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></emphasis></para>
<title>Meanings in Functions</title>
<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
<para>The number of cubic yards of dirt, \(D\), needed to cover a garden with an area of \(a\) square feet is given by \(D=g(a)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>A garden with area 5000 \(ft^2\) requires 50 \(yd^3\) of dirt. Express this information in terms of the function \(g\).</item>
  <item>Explain the meaning of the statement \(g(100)=1\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Solution</emphasis></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(g(5000)=50\)</item>
  <item>The number of cubic yards of dirt required for a garden of 100 square feet is 1.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
<para>Let \(f(t)\) be the number of ducks in a lake \(t\) years after 1990. Explain the meaning of each statement:</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\(f(5)=30\)</item>
  <item>\(f(10)=40\)</item>
</list>
<emphasis><para>Solution</para></emphasis>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>In 1995 (5 years after 1990), there were 30 ducks in the lake.</item>
  <item>In 2000 (10 years after 1990), there were 40 ducks in the lake.</item>
</list>
<title>Try It: Meanings in Functions</title>
<para>The amount of garbage, \(G\), produced by a city with population \(p\) is given by \(G=f(p)\). \(G\) is measured in tons per week, and \(p\) is measured in thousands of people.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>The town of Tola has a population of 40,000 and produces 13 tons of garbage each week. Express this information in terms of the function \(f\).</item>
  <para>\(13=f(40)\)</para>
  <item>Explain the meaning of the statement \(f(5)=2\).</item>
  <para>Five thousand people will produce 2 tons of garbage each week.</para>

</list>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
</section>
</content>
